# Linux CLI Basics

## Terminal Basics
- Terminal is a text-based interface to interact with Linux
- Faster and gives more control than GUI
- Used heavily in cybersecurity tools (e.g., Kali Linux)

## Check Current Directory
- Command: pwd
- Shows present working directory
- Displays current location in systems

---

# List Files and Directorie

## ls Commands
- Command: ls
- Shows files/folders in current directory

## Detailed View
- Command: ls -l
- Shows permissions, size, date

## Hidden Files
- Command: ls -a
- Shows hidden files (starting with .

---

# Directory Navigation

## Change Directory
- Command: cd <directory>
- Move into a directory

## Move Back
- Command: cd ..
- Move one level up

## Example
- cd Documents
- cd ..

---

# Find Files

## Command
- find <path> -name <filename>

## Example
- find ~ -name mission_brief.txt

## Use
- Locate files in system
- Returns full file path

---

# Read File Content

## Command
- cat <filename>

## Example
- cat mission_brief.txt

## Use
- Displays file content in terminal

---

# User Information

## Command
- whoami
- Shows currently logged-in user

---

# System Information

## Full Details
- Command: uname -a
- Shows kernel, architecture, system details

## OS Name Only
- Command: uname
- Shows OS name

---

# Disk Usage

## Command
- df -h
- Shows storage in human-readable format (GB/MB)

## Terms
- Size → Total space
- Used → Used space
- Avail → Free space

---

# Important Directories

## /home
- User files

## /etc
- System configuration files

---

# Read System Files

## Navigate
- cd /etc

## View File
- cat os-release

## Contains
- OS version
- OS name
- ID

---

# Hidden Files
- Start with .
- Not visible with normal ls
- Use ls -a to view

---

# Mini Workflow

## Steps
- Find file → find
- Navigate → cd
- Verify → ls
- Read → cat
